## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Observed 2026-08-30 by the B056 lane while pinning the module-name scope rule.
Recorded as an observation, not acted on: no corpus row depends on it, and it
was found mid-gate on an unrelated ticket.

## Observation

`CMwNod` behaves as a live AngelScript scope in the game, but
`GlobalScope::scope_exists` answers false for it. Same shape as the
`GameData` exception B056 documented, except in CLASS position rather than
module position.

## Why it may matter

B056 established, and the coordinator verified independently against all 20
Nadeo module names, that:

    a module name is a live scope  <=>  Core declares it as a namespace

with `GameData` the single unexplained exception (4 scopes: Audio, Meta, Net,
GameData; Audio/Meta/Net are Core namespaces, GameData is not). `CMwNod` is a
SECOND unexplained live scope, in a different position, which weakens the
'lone exception' reading — two exceptions in two positions is more likely a
missing rule than two quirks.

If the rule is actually something like 'a name is a scope when the engine
registers members under it', then both `GameData` and `CMwNod` may be
explained by the same mechanism, and `scope_exists` is under-approximating
rather than these being anomalies. Worth finding out before either is treated
as permanent.

## Work

Probe first, implement second. Establish what IS a scope in the live game
beyond the 20 module names — at minimum a paired `ZZZNoSuchEnumName` probe
across a sample of engine CLASS names, so the class-position rule is measured
rather than assumed from one observation.

Then decide whether `scope_exists` should recognise them. Note the
consequence direction: `scope_exists` answering false where the game says true
is a FALSE POSITIVE risk (the LSP rejects a spelling the game accepts), which
is the more damaging direction and the one B052/B056/B057 exist to remove.

Do NOT hardcode names. The B056 lane declined to match `GameData` for exactly
that reason — hardcoding an engine fact neither database records is how this
repo's earlier wrong numbers were made.

## Test plan

Whatever lands must pin the measured set the way
`a_module_name_is_a_scope_only_when_core_declares_it` does: assert the split
AND the exact count of exceptions, so a new exception goes red rather than
quietly widening what qualifies.

## Related

B056 (established the module-name rule and its one exception), B052
(ancestor-walk / scope gating), `docs/probes/b052-qualified-enum-spellings.tsv`
(the paired-probe corpus; extend it rather than starting a new one).

## MEASURED (coordinator, 2026-08-31) — this ticket's premise is WRONG

Ran the class-position campaign the Work section asked for, against the live
game (Openplanet 1.29.5 next/Public/1234ad9e). Corpus:
`docs/probes/b067-class-position-scopes.tsv`.

**`CMwNod` is not an exception. Every engine class is a live scope.**

41 engine classes sampled deterministically (seed 67), spread across all 20
modules, deliberately split between types that HAVE nested enums (`e` key, 134
in the dump) and types that do NOT (2451). **All 41 answered SCOPE** — the
`Identifier 'ZZZNoSuchName' is not a data type in namespace 'X' or parent`
discriminator, not `Namespace 'X' doesn't exist.` Having nested enums makes no
difference, so the hypothesis "a class is a scope when members are registered
under it" is refuted: classes with nothing under them are scopes too.

### The rule

    X is a live scope for `X::Y`  <=>  X is a declared namespace OR X is a known data type

Both halves measured, in isolated compilation units (see B068 for why that
matters):

| probe | verdict | evidence |
|---|---|---|
| `ZZZNoSuchClass::` | NOT a scope | `Namespace 'ZZZNoSuchClass' doesn't exist.` |
| `MwFoundations::` `Game::` `ShootMania::` | NOT scopes | same message — confirms B056's module rule |
| `Audio::` `Net::` `Meta::` `GameData::` | scopes | the 4 known live module scopes |
| `CMwNod::` `CGameAction::` `CAudioListener::` | scopes | re-verified individually |
| `string::` | scope | a VALUE type, not an engine class — so the rule is about types generally |
| `CMwNod` / `string` in type position | ARE data types | accepted as a declaration |

This is ordinary AngelScript: `Type::Nested` is scope-resolution syntax, so any
known type name opens a scope regardless of whether anything is in it.

### B056's exception SURVIVES, and is not paired with this

I tested the tempting unification — "GameData is a scope because it is secretly
a type" — and it is **false**:

    Identifier 'GameData' is not a data type in global namespace

Same for `Audio`, `Net`, `Meta`. So `GameData` is a scope while being neither a
Core-declared namespace (B056) nor a data type. It remains the single
unexplained module-position exception. The ticket's reasoning that "two
exceptions in two positions is more likely a missing rule" was right that a
rule was missing — but the missing rule explains `CMwNod` and every other type,
not `GameData`.

### Consequence for the fix

`scope_exists` under-approximates, and in the FALSE POSITIVE direction the
ticket correctly flags as the damaging one. It should answer true when the name
is a **known type in the loaded index**, not only when it is a namespace. That
is derivable from the index itself, so it satisfies "do NOT hardcode names" —
there is nothing to hardcode.

Scope note: this is `src/typedb/index.rs:707` and
`src/typecheck/global_scope.rs:481`. `index.rs` is lane-b057's file for the
B056 follow-up, so **this ticket stays blocked until that lane lands**, and
whoever takes it should read lane-b057's resolution of where the
`scope_exists(ns)` gate sits before touching it.

### Honest limits

- 41 of 2585 types is a sample, not a census. All 41 agreed and the mechanism
  explains why it should be universal, but it is a sample. A census is cheap if
  ever wanted.
- The 41 came from one batch. That batch is trustworthy **because all 41
  produced distinct diagnostics** — a parse abort (B068) yields zero, not some.
  Three were re-verified in isolation regardless.
- Measured on one install, one Openplanet build. A probe bounds behaviour, not
  intent.
- The follow-up run to re-verify the B052 corpus could not complete: the game
  exited mid-run. See B068.


## Investigation result (lane-b057, 2026-08-31): NOT A DEFECT — `scope_exists` does not under-approximate

Measured agreement between the LSP and the game on every corpus row that
exercises a single-segment engine-class qualifier: **25 agree, 0 disagree**.
`scope_exists` is already correct. No production change was made and none is
needed.

### The rule

A class qualifies a globally visible enum **iff Openplanet registered at least
one member enum under it — named OR anonymous.** Openplanet registers a
class's enums by setting the default AngelScript namespace to the class name,
so having an enum to register is what creates the class-named namespace. With
no enum there is no namespace, `::` resolves against the class TYPE and its
base classes only, and the walk never reaches global.

This rule was already documented at `src/typedb/index.rs:456-464`, and
`build_scope_index`'s Nadeo arm already implements it by deriving scopes from
enum qnames. The investigation confirmed the documentation rather than
correcting it.

| class | member enums (named / anonymous) | scope_exists | game |
|---|---|---|---|
| CGameCtnChallenge | 3 named | true | ACCEPTED |
| CGameMenu | 1 named (`EMenuOrder`) | true | ACCEPTED |
| CGameCtnApp | **0 named, 1 anonymous** | true | ACCEPTED |
| CMwNod | none | false | REJECTED |
| CSceneVehicleVis | none | false | REJECTED |
| CSystemFidFile | none | false | REJECTED |
| CTrackMania | none | false | REJECTED |

Namespace-hood is NOT inherited: `CTrackMania -> CGameManiaPlanet ->
CGameCtnApp -> CGameApp -> CMwNod`, and `CGameCtnApp` is a namespace while its
descendant `CTrackMania` is not. The default namespace attaches to the class
that declares the enum, not to the subtree.

### Two measurement errors worth recording, because both nearly closed this wrong

The ticket's original premise ("CMwNod is a live game scope but `scope_exists`
answers false") and the coordinator's re-framing ("every engine class is a
scope, 41/41") were both refuted by the **12 REJECTED corpus rows** — the
direction B068 cannot corrupt. Widening `scope_exists` would have turned 12
correct rejections into false negatives. The discriminator used for the 41/41
measurement was the game's rejection MESSAGE FORM, which separates *head
recognised* from *head unknown* and says nothing about whether lookup through
that head reaches a global enum: `CMwNod::ZZZNoSuchName` and
`CMwNod::VirtualKey` produce the same form, yet `CGameCtnApp::VirtualKey`
ACCEPTS and `CMwNod::VirtualKey` REJECTS.

Then, while confirming the surviving rule, two further errors landed on the
same fact from opposite sides:

1. **Reading enum-TYPED MEMBERS as declared enums.** `VehicleCollectionId`,
   `Kind`, `Difficulty`, `ColorPalette`, `MenuOrder` are fields whose type is
   an enum, not enums. Counting members that carry an inline `e` block gave a
   perfect 7/7 correlation — for a reason the author did not understand and
   described incorrectly.
2. **Counting only the class-level `e` array.** That array lists NAMED enums
   only. Anonymous inline enums are keyed by declaring context via
   `anon_member_enum_key` (`src/typedb/index.rs:1123-1136`), because the
   `UnnamedEnum` placeholder is shared across a module and keying by it
   collapsed 118 of the fixture's 132 into one `or_insert` (B016). So this
   count reads 0 for `CGameCtnApp` while the index does hold an enum under it,
   and suggested a 3-row disagreement that does not exist.

Error 1 had wrong labels and the right quantity; error 2 had right labels and
an incomplete quantity. The first nearly closed the ticket on a rule stated
wrongly; the second nearly held it open pending a re-probe of `CGameCtnApp::*`
that would not have flipped. Neither was caught by re-reading the reasoning —
both were caught by measuring the loaded index directly.

### The actual answer to what this ticket asked

B067 was filed to find out whether these were anomalies or a missing rule. They
are neither: the rule exists, is documented, and is implemented. What the
investigation did surface is a real structural hazard worth more than the
close.

**The codebase carries two scope predicates and two enum predicates, and the
bugs live where a caller picks the wrong one.**

- `TypeIndex::scope_exists` (index-only) vs `GlobalScope::scope_exists`
  (workspace OR index). B070 was a surface reaching the index-only half, so
  hover was silent on a spelling the checker accepts.
- `has_enum` (an ACCEPTANCE test) vs `canonical_enum_key` (a CANONICALIZER,
  free to be wider than acceptance because every other caller reaches it only
  after acceptance). B070's first fix reached the canonicalizer as though it
  were an acceptance test and made hover answer for 24 spellings the checker
  rejects, all `Plot::X` matching the real key `UI::Plot::X`.

Opposite ends of one family: a surface using a predicate that is too narrow,
and a surface using one that is too wide. Both are invisible at the call site,
because both pairs have near-identical names and plausible-looking semantics.

Exhaustive check by search, not by reasoning: `source_reference_key` has
exactly ONE production caller (`src/server/hover.rs`, fixed by B070), and the
index-only `scope_exists` has exactly ONE (`src/typecheck/global_scope.rs:494`,
the correct workspace-OR-index composition). No other surface currently carries
the defect. B063 and B069 are adjacent but reach it through different
functions.

### Recommendation

Close as **not a defect**. Do not widen `scope_exists`. If the two-predicates
hazard is to be acted on, it wants its own ticket — naming the pairs so a
future caller cannot pick the wrong one by accident is the fix, not a
behavioural change.


### Refinement to error 2 (coordinator, verified independently 2026-08-31)

The coordinator ran two checks and both missed the anonymous enum for the same
root cause, which is worth recording because the second looked like an
independent confirmation of the first:

1. Counting the class-level `e` array — lists NAMED enums only, so 0 for
   `CGameCtnApp`.
2. Falling back to "does the class have an enum-TYPED member?" — this *would*
   have caught it, except the anonymous enum's type is the shared
   `UnnamedEnum` placeholder, which never matches a set of named enum types.

Two checks, one blind spot, both confidently wrong in the same direction. A
second check that shares the first's population assumption is not an
independent check.

Independent confirmation against the live dump, members carrying an inline
enum block:

```
CGameCtnApp        named-e=0  inline-enum members=1  ['VehicleCollectionId']
CGameMenu          named-e=1  inline-enum members=1
CGameCtnChallenge  named-e=3  inline-enum members=4
CMwNod / CSceneVehicleVis / CSystemFidFile / CTrackMania   both zero
```

7/7, no outlier. The re-probe prediction that `CGameCtnApp::*` would flip to
REJECTED was retracted before it consumed game-port time.

### Closing note

Closed as **not a defect**. `scope_exists` does not under-approximate;
agreement with the game is 25/25 measured against the loaded index. Both
investigators reached a wrong intermediate conclusion about the same fact from
opposite directions, and in both cases the source had already recorded the
answer at `src/typedb/index.rs:456-464`. The durable lesson is not who was
right: it is that a derived measurement was trusted over a documented one
twice in the same investigation.